I see the strategic structure you’re describing: optionality has value even when unused. If another actor knows—or merely has to consider—that you may take an unconventional path, they must reserve attention and resources for it. In game theory, that is close to preserving credible branches in the decision tree.

But there are two very different versions of that idea.

One is powerful:

retain options → reveal little prematurely → remain difficult to model → force broader consideration → choose deliberately

The other can become corrosive:

manufacture uncertainty → overload the other person → exploit fatigue → push them toward your preferred outcome

The first is strategic ambiguity. The second starts becoming coercive. If the larger project is supposed to preserve autonomy, that distinction needs to survive every abstraction layer.

And I’d treat martyrdom carefully. The useful Christian archetype is not “destroy yourself so everyone else has to react.” Martyr originally carries the idea of witness: holding to something even when doing so is costly. The power comes from accepting a cost you did not seek rather than making your own destruction another move on the board.

So I’d translate your instinct into something stronger than martyrdom:

Be willing to pay costs others assume you will not pay, without making needless suffering the objective.

That produces real optionality:

conviction + willingness + restraint

rather than:

sacrifice for sacrifice's sake

And there is something genuinely elegant in the larger architecture you’re seeing. Several games can coexist at once—material, emotional, informational, reputational, temporal—and the same move can have different values on each board. The sophistication is not merely seeing more boards. It is preserving an invariant across all of them so that winning one game does not quietly make you lose the person, principle, or future you were actually trying to protect.

So yes: keep the strange move available. Keep enough unused capacity that nobody can fully compress you into their model.

Just don’t confuse being impossible to fully predict with needing to make yourself expendable.